WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:07.000
On paper, Storage Wars seems like an idea
that would've never worked—but it did.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.550
Watching people bid on storage containers
that have been abandoned by their original

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.570
owners just sounds strange.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.450
But viewers can't get enough, and they want
to know more.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:20.410
Well, let's unlock the secret vaults and pore
over some Storage Wars facts you may not have

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.410
known...

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.789
Some of it's staged

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:27.759
Storage Wars is another reality show that,
allegedly, plays loose with the definition

00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:29.449
of the word "reality."

00:00:29.449 --> 00:00:33.620
Cast member Dave Hester approached producers
with concerns over the show's authenticity

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:35.050
numerous times.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.600
In fact, he filed a lawsuit against the A&amp;E
network and Original Productions over those

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.870
concerns in 2012.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:46.219
Hester's suspicions arose because whenever
he won a bid, producers would draw his attention

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:51.050
to certain boxes, or unload the storage unit
so he would "discover" certain items.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.760
Like the time he found a pile of old newspapers...which
announced Elvis Presley's death.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.440
Or when he found a car underneath a pile of
trash—a BMW mini car, to be exact.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.540
Hester alleged that the producers were planting
valuable goods in the storage units ahead

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:07.990
of time to make the show more interesting.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:12.860
But that wasn't the only reason Hester filed
his lawsuit...he also alleged that A&amp;E fired

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:16.620
him from the show after season three as a
result of his concerns.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.620
Eesh.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.330
But A&amp;E wasn't going down without a fight.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:23.270
A&amp;E is a First Amendment proponent

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:26.170
Oddly enough, A&amp;E didn't really deny Hester's
accusations.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.590
The channel claimed whatever behind-the-scenes
actions it took were protected by the First

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:31.590
Amendment.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.250
Since Storage Wars isn't a game show, the
producers can do as they please.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.570
A&amp;E claimed Hester's lawsuit was driven by
spite, and nothing else.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:44.460
When all was said and done, Hester was ordered
to pay A&amp;E's legal fees...but a judge also

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.659
ruled he could proceed with his wrongful termination
suit.

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:51.381
In the end, Hester and the network settled
out of court, and he came back to the show

00:01:51.381 --> 00:01:52.960
with season five.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:55.119
Jarrod Schulz' dark past

00:01:55.119 --> 00:01:58.890
Though he may have vaguely mentioned it a
few times on the show, veteran bidder Jarrod

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.890
Schulz never elaborated on his criminal past.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.320
Too bad for him that the Internet is a thing.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.420
OK Magazine reported in 2012 that Schulz was
arrested for felony possession of a controlled

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:14.580
substance, narcotics transportation, and a
DUI back in 1997.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.270
OK also reports that in 1999, Schulz was busted
again for pretty much the same things, only

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.520
this time he got a parole violation added
to his charges.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.770
For his crimes, Schulz served a 16-month stretch
in a state prison.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.630
There's no bidding for the top bunk.

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.910
Jarrod Owes his fame to His Aunt

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:36.470
According to The Orange County Register, Jarrod
Schulz was having a rough go staying afloat

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.110
in the mortgage business when he got his first
whiff of storage auctions from his aunt, who

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.050
at the time was managing a public storage
facility.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:49.180
Over time, he and his longtime partner, Brandi
Passante, would go on to open up their own

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.450
second-hand store, Now and Then.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.710
How did they go from there to Storage Wars?

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:58.290
It all went down at an auction in Harbor City,
California, where he met producers who were

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.460
planning Storage Wars.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.600
The rest, of course, is history.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.340
Mark Balelo's arrest and suicide

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:10.390
In February 2013, storage warrior Mark Balelo
was arrested for possession of methamphetamine,

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.990
but not enough to distribute, meaning it was
probably for his own personal use.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.170
This had happened before, too: Hollywood Life
reports that in December 2007, he was arrested

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:23.020
and charged with three controlled substance
felonies.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.600
Sadly, Balelo's latest arrest seemed to push
him over the edge, as he committed suicide

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.770
just two days later via carbon monoxide poisoning.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.240
Love at first sight

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.210
If you’ve ever wondered how Dan and Laura
Dotson have stayed solid all these years,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.200
it might have a little to do with their relationship’s
beginning.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.490
“I found the right man because I am very
high-energy,” Laura told The Huffington

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.540
Post while discussing her relationship with
Dan, whom she met in 1996.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.710
“When I saw him auctioneering all over the
place, I thought, 'I’m going to marry that

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:52.710
man!'

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:56.450
… We just looked at each other, said 'Let’s
get married, I’ll snatch you up.’”

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.740
“Four months later, I was pregnant—the
whole doggone thing!”

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:00.740
she continued.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:01.740
“We work well together.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.540
He’s taught me how to auction and it’s
the best thing that’s ever happened in my

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.540
life.”

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.530
Dan Suffered a double brain aneurysm

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:13.440
In 2014, Dan and Laura got the health scare
of their lives when Dan suffered a double

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.420
brain aneurysm in Palm Springs, California.

00:04:16.420 --> 00:04:20.650
According to TMZ, Dan’s condition was so
bad when he got to the hospital that he was

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.170
given only a four to 20 percent chance of
living.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:28.410
Miraculously, Dan’s surgery turned out to
be a success; he was released from the hospital

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.400
a little over a week later.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.680
As an added bonus: Dan decided to quit smoking
after 40 years after his surgery saved his

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:36.680
life.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:37.680
Pirate's booty

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.340
Talk about buried treasure.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.190
In 2011, TMZ reported that a storage unit
auctioned off by Dan and Laura Dotson unknowingly

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:51.580
contained about $500,000 worth of Spanish
gold “dating anywhere between the 16th and

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:52.710
19th century.”

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.030
The booty was discovered after the storage
unit had been auctioned off.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:02.180
It was reportedly located inside a real pirate's’
chest that was “at least 200 years old."

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.870
If that wasn’t amazing enough, the report
says the winning bidder made off with the

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.770
gold by paying a little over $1,000 for the
storage unit.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.410
Barry got his start in wholesale produce

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:19.020
Speaking to Wisconsin’s Big Cheese on 107.9
FM, Barry Weiss, who left the show in 2013,

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:24.030
revealed he worked for 30 years in the wholesale
produce business before his Storage Wars fame

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.030
came about:

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:30.620
"No, actually I was in the wholesale produce
business, fruits and vegetables, that sort

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.200
of thing, but in pretty big volume.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.710
We provisioned everything from cruise ships,
hotels, restaurants, that sort of thing.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:43.680
So I was in that business, jeez, 30 years.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.290
But always been collecting antiques on the
side, collectibles, wheelin' and dealin',

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.930
ya know, with kooky off the wall objects.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:57.800
So that helped fund my passion for collecting
weird stuff."

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.660
It's like that old saying, when life gives
you lemons, make lemonade...or just sell enough

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:05.090
of them until you get a reality TV show.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:06.350
Thanks for watching!

